JS_ToCStringLen2: the string is freed when its UTF-8 cannot be allocated

Jahshaka's change, MCP-ON-HOST-1 stage 2 (2026-11-23). quickjs-ng at the pinned
v0.18.0 (d7215b0), file quickjs.c, JS_ToCStringLen2.

The bug
-------
js_force_tostring makes (or dups) the string into val. Both `goto fail` after a
refused js_alloc_string (the 8-bit path that widens non-ASCII bytes, :4902, and
the 16-bit path, :4920) return WITHOUT JS_FreeValue(ctx, val): the string stays
referenced for the runtime's life. Under a memory limit (Host::Options::
memoryLimit) a refused conversion therefore shrinks the heap for good: the kept
reproduction (SCRIPT-IN-ENGINE-1 2b, spikes/script-in-engine-1/stage2b/
quickjs-tocstring-leak-repro.txt) - a 1.5 MiB U+00E9 string whose UTF-8 is
refused under 4 MiB, then a 3 MiB buffer is refused too.

The fix
-------
JS_FreeValue(ctx, val) at the fail label: every failure frees what the function
took. On the first failure (js_force_tostring itself threw) val is JS_EXCEPTION,
which frees nothing.

Its law (script.host case 12d (bis), ours): the refused conversion FAILS its
command by name "out of memory", and the next run allocates 3 MiB of the 4 MiB.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -4952,6 +4952,10 @@
         *plen = str_new->len;
     return (const char *)str8(str_new);
 fail:
+    /* JAHSHAKA 0002 (irisgl/thirdparty/quickjs-ng-patches): the string js_force_tostring made (or dup'd) is freed on
+       every failure - js_alloc_string refused past the runtime's memory limit left it referenced for the runtime's
+       life (on the first failure val is JS_EXCEPTION: freeing it is nothing) */
+    JS_FreeValue(ctx, val);
     if (plen)
         *plen = 0;
     return NULL;
